WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.269
How chips are

00:00:01.081 --> 00:00:02.331
really designed in

00:00:02.142 --> 00:00:02.838
the open

00:00:03.366 --> 00:00:04.910
Vitalik Buterin wrote

00:00:04.722 --> 00:00:05.653
a primer on

00:00:05.465 --> 00:00:06.467
open source chip

00:00:06.279 --> 00:00:07.410
design for people

00:00:07.222 --> 00:00:07.929
used to a

00:00:07.741 --> 00:00:08.990
pure software view

00:00:08.802 --> 00:00:09.675
of computing

00:00:09.816 --> 00:00:10.877
with review from

00:00:10.689 --> 00:00:11.538
Matt Venn

00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:13.154
Like software

00:00:13.308 --> 00:00:14.475
hardware is written

00:00:14.287 --> 00:00:15.112
in high level

00:00:14.924 --> 00:00:15.666
languages

00:00:15.785 --> 00:00:16.928
then compiled into

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:17.907
progressively lower

00:00:17.719 --> 00:00:18.426
levels

00:00:18.565 --> 00:00:19.803
His example circuit

00:00:19.614 --> 00:00:20.805
simply multiplies a

00:00:20.617 --> 00:00:21.795
state by three

00:00:21.607 --> 00:00:22.786
on every clock

00:00:22.598 --> 00:00:23.140
tick

00:00:23.678 --> 00:00:24.562
At the bottom

00:00:24.798 --> 00:00:26.119
layouts say exactly

00:00:25.930 --> 00:00:26.932
which layer gets

00:00:26.744 --> 00:00:27.852
which rectangle

00:00:28.018 --> 00:00:29.314
and process names

00:00:29.126 --> 00:00:30.942
like 180 or

00:00:30.753 --> 00:00:32.498
130 refer to

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:33.961
nanometers of transistor

00:00:33.772 --> 00:00:34.703
gate length

00:00:35.238 --> 00:00:36.252
Full story on

00:00:36.063 --> 00:00:36.959
the dashboard
